  "surprise": "Value embeddings (six per-block (107, 256) token-identity tables added to V before the KV-cache write, modded-nanogpt style; 4,964,352 params as registered) on 115-fable scored 632/630/624 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 115's 639/646/609: exact means 629.00 vs 631.33, -2.33, paired -7/-16/+15, inside the registered -35..+55 range but below its central +15. Discard. The registered mechanism (a better round-1 RL start from a clean lexical value channel) is NOT supported: round-1 targets solved 670/744/506 (mean 640) vs 115's 718/756/691 (722), round-1 sample acc 0.264/0.302/0.150 vs 0.318 on 115 seed 0, and seed 2 opened with 23,680 of 48,000 round-1 samples failing to parse (49%, the same pathology as 104-claude's seed 2) before fine-tuning cut that to 10% and lifted it from 506 to 909 in round 2. Expert iteration then erased the whole gap: round-4 targets solved 1128/1109/1081 (mean 1106) vs 115's 1131/1122/1065 (mean 1106), identical, and the eval length histograms sit on top of each other (>=9: 431/431/430 vs 115 seed 0's 442; >=10: 92/92/96 vs 103), so whatever the tables changed, the fixed RL recipe converged to the same policy. Surprise: the tables cost far more than the predicted <=3%. Pretraining ran 3917/3881/3871 steps vs 4204/4218/4263 (-8%), and sampling fell to 174-218 samples/s in RL rounds and 181-196 in eval vs 115's 256-296, so RL rounds took 284-350 s vs 238-261 s and totals were 1947/1987/1959 s vs 1572 s (+25%): six extra lookup/reshape/add launches per block per decoded token on the launch-bound fork sampler (cf. 109-fable Canon, 111-fable NoPE's +44% from removing RoPE's small ops), though part of the sampling gap may be pod variance. Fit was unchanged per second and slightly better per step: final val loss 0.0799/0.0823/0.0846 (mean 0.0823) vs 115's 0.0826 mean on 8% fewer steps, held-out greedy 0.979/0.983/0.9805 vs 0.9815/0.983/0.9845, eval-time greedy 0.99/0.986/0.989. Lean vetoed 0 nd_verify-accepted proofs; 1/4/8 Lean-accepted proofs were nd_verify-rejected. Reading: on this 6-block, 107-token model the residual stream already carries token identity well enough for the retrieval heads; a direct token-to-value path adds nothing the RL stage can use, and its per-token kernel cost is a liability at this budget. Next fable idea should be something that changes the round-1 sample distribution (accuracy or parse rate on 7+ line targets), since that is where 115's edge over its predecessors came from and where every recent architecture nudge has been flat.",
